//Upcasting and Downcasting

Casting between a parent (base) class and child (derived) class is called upcasting/downcasting.

Upcasting

Converting a child object → parent pointer/reference.

class Animal {
};

class Dog : public Animal {
};

Dog d;
Animal* a = &d;

Here:

Dog → Animal
Child → Parent

This is upcasting.

It is generally safe and commonly used in runtime polymorphism.

Downcasting

Converting a parent pointer/reference → child pointer/reference.

Animal* a = new Dog();

Dog* d = dynamic_cast<Dog*>(a);

Here:

Animal → Dog
Parent → Child

This is downcasting.

dynamic_cast can safely check at runtime whether the object is actually of the derived type.

Simple definition

Upcasting = Child → Parent
Downcasting = Parent → Child